        "Carbon dioxide's own molar mass"
      ],
      "reveals": {
        "secondary": "The moving packet marks a calculation step",
        "callout": "Carbon dioxide's own molar mass"
      },
      "diagramBeats": {
        "in": "Carbon's molar mass connects its grams",
        "ratio": "The equation then connects those moles",
        "out": "Carbon dioxide's own molar mass",
        "trap": "These equal piles show the one-to-one amount relationship"
      }
    },
    "definition": {
      "bullets": [
        "the stated reactant amount has to react completely",
        "we'll start with pure iron three oxide",
        "The answer is a theoretical mass"
      ],
      "reveals": {
        "callout": "What we actually collect can be lower"
      }
    },
    "formula": {
      "bullets": [
        "We divide the known mass",
        "We then multiply by the wanted coefficient",
        "Finally the wanted substance's molar mass"
      ],
      "reveals": {
        "secondary": "You can combine the three relationships",
        "callout": "The two numbers might happen to be equal"
      }
    },
    "worked-example": {
      "reveals": {
        "stepAts": [
          "Carbon's molar mass turns",
          "The equation gives one mole",
          "For the mass we need"
        ]
      },
      "stageLines": {
        "0": [
          {
            "text": "n(C) = 12.0 ÷ 12.011",
            "cue": "Carbon's molar mass turns"
          },
          {
            "text": "n(C) ≈ 0.999084 mol",
            "cue": "just under one mole"
          }
        ],
        "1": [
          {
            "text": "CO₂ : C = 1 : 1",
            "cue": "The equation gives one mole"
          },
          {
            "text": "n(CO₂) = n(C) × (1 ÷ 1)",
            "cue": "so the product amount is the same"
          }
        ],
        "2": [
          {
            "text": "M(CO₂) = 12.011 + 2(15.999) = 44.009 g mol⁻¹",
            "cue": "It includes one carbon and two oxygens"
          },
          {
            "text": "m(CO₂) = (12.0 ÷ 12.011) × 44.009",
            "cue": "Multiplying by that molar mass gives"
          },
          {
            "text": "m(CO₂) ≈ 43.969 g → 44.0 g",
            "cue": "forty-four point zero grams"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "worked-example-2": {
      "reveals": {
        "stepAts": [
          "The oxide's own molar mass",
          "One mole of oxide produces two moles of iron",
          "Now iron's molar mass"
        ]
      },
      "stageLines": {
        "0": [
          {
            "text": "M(Fe₂O₃) = 2(55.845) + 3(15.999) = 159.687 g mol⁻¹",
            "cue": "The oxide's own molar mass"
          },
          {
            "text": "n(Fe₂O₃) = 80.0 ÷ 159.687 ≈ 0.500980 mol",
            "cue": "just over half a mole of oxide"
          }
        ],
        "1": [
          {
            "text": "Fe : Fe₂O₃ = 2 : 1",
            "cue": "One mole of oxide produces two moles of iron"
          },
          {
            "text": "n(Fe) = n(Fe₂O₃) × (2 ÷ 1) ≈ 1.001960 mol",
            "cue": "so the iron amount doubles"
          }
        ],
        "2": [
          {
            "text": "m(Fe) = (80.0 ÷ 159.687) × 2 × 55.845",
            "cue": "Now iron's molar mass"
          },
          {
            "text": "m(Fe) ≈ 55.954 g → 56.0 g",
            "cue": "fifty-six point zero grams"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    "misconception": {
      "reveals": {
        "body": "Carbon to carbon dioxide was one to one",
        "secondary": "Oxide to iron was one to two",
        "callout": "Keep unrounded values in the calculation"
      }
    },
